19529 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419529 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419529 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186419459 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419529 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d89223e5-0c7d-5154-95e1-d5f1014440e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419530 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186419448 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419530 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186419465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419530 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186419494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419530 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186419500 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419530 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186419508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419530 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419530 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419530 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186419448 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419530 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "eda912e0-867b-5e29-86d2-2c6727a332af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419531 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419531 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131277" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419531 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419531 #edb/ref 1071 "The Philippines is subject to tropical cyclones, with a month-round average frequency of $2.5$ typhoons per month. What is the probability that there will be no more than $2$ typhoons next month? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that typhoons occur independently and at a constant average rate." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419531 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1343b72e-5bbd-541b-8ed3-38578a085bf3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419531 #edb/ref 1074 "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of typhoons in a month in the Philippines. Since the average rate is $2.5$ typhoons per month, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of typhoons can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =2.5,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(2.5).$ \n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{2.5}^{x}{e}^{-2.5}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that there will be at most $2$ typhoons next month is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =P(X\\in {0,1,2}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =\\frac{{2.5}^{0}{e}^{-2.5}}{0!}+\\frac{{2.5}^{1}{e}^{-2.5}}{1!}+\\frac{{2.5}^{2}{e}^{-2.5}}{2!} \\\\  & ≈0.544\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419532 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419532 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131266" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419532 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419532 #edb/ref 1071 "Large metal sheets have faults in random positions but, on average, there is one fault per $10\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.$ What is the probability that the next sheet of size $5\\,\\text{m}\\times 8\\,\\text{m}$ will have exactly one fault? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that faults occur independently and at a constant average rate." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419532 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "15726506-9b26-5fcb-965d-d1bfeaeef6e9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419532 #edb/ref 1074 "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of faults in the next sheet of area $40\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}\\,(5\\,\\text{m}\\times 8\\,\\text{m}).$ Since there is on average $1$ fault per  $10\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}$, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, we expect $4$ faults in a sheet of size $40\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.$ So, the number of faults in a $5\\,\\text{m}\\times 8\\,\\text{m}$ metal sheet can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =4,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(4).$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{4}^{x}{e}^{-4}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the next $5\\,\\text{m}\\times 8\\,\\text{m}$ metal sheet will have exactly $1$ fault is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=1) & =f(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{4}^{1}{e}^{-4}}{1!} \\\\  & ≈0.073\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419533 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419516 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419533 #edb/ref 1069 "q-85317" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419533 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419533 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following random variables could be modeled using a Poisson distribution?\n\n- The number of defective televisions manufactured by a company in a month\n- The grade point average of a college class\n- The average speed of a car during a trip" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419533 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2963275e-8991-5613-bcc4-d15bd0b82d44" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419533 #edb/ref 1074 "A Poisson distribution models the number of events that occur in an interval of time or space if events occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I follows a Poisson distribution. The number of defective televisions can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know how many events occur in an interval of time (a month).\n\n- Random variable II does *not* follow a Poisson distribution. The grade point average of a college class is not an event that occurs at a constant average rate.\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow a Poisson distribution. The average speed of a car is not an event that occurs at a constant average rate.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419534 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419523 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419534 #edb/ref 1069 "q-89244" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419534 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419534 #edb/ref 1071 "On average, a wedding dress store sells $9$ wedding dresses per week. What is the probability that the wedding dress store will sell more than $3$ wedding dresses next week? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that wedding dress sales occur independently and at a constant average rate." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419534 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2a680a24-a0a8-52d9-a8a7-fff48b164489" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419534 #edb/ref 1074 "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of wedding dresses sold. Since the average rate is $9$ wedding dresses sold per week, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of wedding dresses sold can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =9,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(9).$ \n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{9}^{x}{e}^{-9}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X>3),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X>3)=1-P(X\\le 3)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in {0,1,2,3}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{{9}^{0}{e}^{-9}}{0!}+\\frac{{9}^{1}{e}^{-9}}{1!}+\\frac{{9}^{2}{e}^{-9}}{2!}+\\frac{{9}^{3}{e}^{-9}}{3!} \\\\  & ≈0.021\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the probability of selling more than $3$ wedding dresses next week is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>3) & =1-P(X\\le 3) \\\\  & ≈1-0.021 \\\\  & =0.979\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419535 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419529 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419535 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131311" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419535 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419535 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following random variables could be modeled using a Poisson distribution?\n\n- The number of network failures per week\n- The number of emergency calls received by a hospital every minute\n- The number of members of a band" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419535 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2a75eec7-e20c-53a7-afe2-6cf4faf6becf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419535 #edb/ref 1074 "A Poisson distribution models the number of events that occur in an interval of time or space if events occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I follows a Poisson distribution. The number of network failures can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know how many events occur in an interval of time (a week).\n\n- Random variable II follows a Poisson distribution.  The number of emergency calls received can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know how many events occur in an interval of time (a minute).\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow a Poisson distribution. There are no events that occur at a constant average rate, and there is no interval of time or space.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419536 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419536 #edb/ref 1069 "q-85325" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419536 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419536 #edb/ref 1071 "A programmer reviews his web page and notices that, on average, $7$ visitors view his page every minute. What is the probability that there will be $5$ visitors in the next minute? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that visitors connect to the site independently and at a constant average rate." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419536 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5019f447-273c-518d-a2e4-ccc53ac4a221" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419536 #edb/ref 1074 "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of visitors to the web page. Since the average rate is $7$ visitors per minute, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of visitors can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =7,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(7).$ \n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{7}^{x}{e}^{-7}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of having $5$ visitors in the next minute is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=5) & =f(5) \\\\  & =\\frac{{7}^{5}{e}^{-7}}{5!} \\\\  & ≈0.128\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419537 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419537 #edb/ref 1069 "q-85390" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419537 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419537 #edb/ref 1071 "On average, every batch of televisions has $5$ defective televisions in a television factory. What is the probability that the factory will produce more than $2$ defective televisions in the next batch? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that defective televisions occur independently and at a constant average rate." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419537 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "61a1ddbb-f5ab-5fff-b498-6bc2ff368bd2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419537 #edb/ref 1074 "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of televisions produced with defects. Since the average rate is $5$ televisions with defects per batch, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of televisions with defects can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =5,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(5).$ \n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{5}^{x}{e}^{-5}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X>2),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X>2)=1-P(X\\le 2)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =P(X\\in {0,1,2}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =\\frac{{5}^{0}{e}^{-5}}{0!}+\\frac{{5}^{1}{e}^{-5}}{1!}+\\frac{{5}^{2}{e}^{-5}}{2!} \\\\  & ≈0.125\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the probability of finding more than $2$ defective televisions in the next batch is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>2) & =1-P(X\\le 2) \\\\  & ≈1-0.125 \\\\  & =0.875\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419538 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419538 #edb/ref 1069 "q-89222" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419538 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419538 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following random variables could be modeled using a Poisson distribution?\n\n- The number of emails received by a company's server in one week\n- The number of people who visit a coffee shop per hour\n- The height of $5$ buildings in New York City" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419538 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "71278ae0-44f4-58f6-827b-4b9773ebb613" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419538 #edb/ref 1074 "A Poisson distribution models the number of events that occur in an interval of time or space if events occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I follows a Poisson distribution. The number of emails received by a company's server can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know how many events occur in an interval of time (one week).\n\n- Random variable II follows a Poisson distribution. The number of people who visit a coffee shop can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know how many events occur in an interval of time (one hour).\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow a Poisson distribution. There are no events that occur at a constant average rate, and there is no interval of time or space.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419539 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419539 #edb/ref 1069 "q-85324" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419539 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419539 #edb/ref 1071 "An airport manager notes that, on average, $4$ planes each day are delayed by more than one hour. What is the probability that precisely $8$ planes will be delayed by more than one hour tomorrow? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that delays occur independently and at a constant average rate." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419539 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7876349f-b735-5751-9a1b-0e3749e49e68" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419539 #edb/ref 1074 "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of airplanes that are delayed by more than one hour on a particular day. Since the average rate is $4$ delayed airplanes per day, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, $X$ can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =4,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(4).$ \n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{4}^{x}{e}^{-4}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of precisely $8$ airplanes will be delayed on any particular day is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=8) & =f(8) \\\\  & =\\frac{{4}^{8}{e}^{-4}}{8!} \\\\  & ≈0.030\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419540 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419519 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419540 #edb/ref 1069 "q-85329" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419540 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419540 #edb/ref 1071 "It is known that, on average, $6$ cell phones per batch produced by a cellphone factory have defects. What is the probability that the next batch contains exactly $4$ phones with defects? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that defective phones occur independently and at a constant average rate." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419540 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "840811f2-e29f-518b-8c90-d58cbee9a3ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419540 #edb/ref 1074 "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of cell phones with defects. Since the average rate is $6$ cell phones per batch, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, $X$ can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =6,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(6).$ \n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{6}^{x}{e}^{-6}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of having $4$ cell phones with defects in the next batch is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=4) & =f(4) \\\\  & =\\frac{{6}^{4}{e}^{-6}}{4!} \\\\  & ≈0.134\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419541 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419515 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419541 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131412" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419541 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419541 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following random variables could be modeled using a Poisson distribution?\n\n- The average points scored on a math test in a class\n- The number of orders a firm receives per month\n- The number of calls a firm will receive next week for help concerning a toy" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419541 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a68d38c1-253b-59e6-ad1e-93208422e4e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419541 #edb/ref 1074 "A Poisson distribution models the number of events that occur in an interval of time or space if events occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I does *not* follow a Poisson distribution. The average points scored on the math test is not an event that occurs at a constant average rate.\n\n- Random variable II follows a Poisson distribution. The number of orders can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know how many events occur in an interval of time (a month).\n\n- Random variable III follows a Poisson distribution. Again, the number of calls can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know how many events occur in an interval of time (a week).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419542 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419542 #edb/ref 1069 "q-85316" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419542 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419542 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following random variables could be modeled using a Poisson distribution?\n\n- The number of visits a web page has per minute\n- The number of works of art created by a sculptor in a period of one year\n- The average height of the players on a basketball team" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419542 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d411b05d-363a-5c38-b187-cabf795e2dff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419542 #edb/ref 1074 "A Poisson distribution models the number of events that occur in an interval of time or space if events occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I follows a Poisson distribution. The number of visits on a web page can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know how many events occur in an interval of time (a minute).\n\n- Random variable II follows a Poisson distribution. The number of works of art can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know how many events occur in an interval of time (a year).\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow a Poisson distribution. There are no events that occur at a constant average rate, and there is no interval of time or space.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419543 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419543 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131431" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419543 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419543 #edb/ref 1071 "The professor of geometry notices that, on average, every batch of practice questions contains $6$ questions that cite the interior angle theorem in the solution. What is the probability that in the next batch of practice questions, there will be more than $5$ questions that cite the interior angle theorem in the solution? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that questions that cite the interior angle theorem in the solution occur independently and at a constant average rate." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419543 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d55083ad-b1c4-5352-954c-42f5ad322bca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419543 #edb/ref 1074 "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of questions that cite the interior angle theorem in the solution. Since the average rate is $6$ questions that cite the interior angle theorem in the solution per batch, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of questions that cite the interior angle theorem in the solution can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =6,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(6).$ \n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{6}^{x}{e}^{-6}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X>5),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X>5)=1-P(X\\le 5)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 5) & =P(X\\in {0,1,2,3,4,5}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4)+f(5) \\\\  & =\\frac{{6}^{0}{e}^{-6}}{0!}+\\frac{{6}^{1}{e}^{-6}}{1!}+\\frac{{6}^{2}{e}^{-6}}{2!}+\\frac{{6}^{3}{e}^{-6}}{3!}+\\frac{{6}^{4}{e}^{-6}}{4!}+\\frac{{6}^{5}{e}^{-6}}{5!} \\\\  & ≈0.446\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the probability of finding more than $5$ questions that cite the interior angle theorem in the solution in the next batch of practice questions is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>5) & =1-P(X\\le 5) \\\\  & ≈1-0.446 \\\\  & =0.554\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419544 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419526 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419544 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131269" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419544 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419544 #edb/ref 1071 "At a dental clinic, patients arrive at an average rate of $10$ patients every hour during the hours when the clinic is open. What is the probability that exactly $5$ customers will arrive in the next hour? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that patients arrive independently and at a constant average rate." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419544 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dddb9707-e886-593b-b435-6f7cb6f00a64" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419544 #edb/ref 1074 "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of patients who will arrive in the next hour. Since the average rate is $10$ patients every hour when the clinic is open, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of patients can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =10,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(10).$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{10}^{x}{e}^{-10}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that exactly $5$ customers will arrive in the next hour while the clinic is open is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=5) & =f(5) \\\\  & =\\frac{{10}^{5}{e}^{-10}}{5!} \\\\  & ≈0.038\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419545 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419545 #edb/ref 1069 "q-89237" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419545 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419545 #edb/ref 1071 "In a certain University, professors report that, on average, out of each group of graduate students, $5$ students graduate with the Summa Cum Laude honor. What is the probability that in the next group of graduate students, no more than $3$ students will have the Summa Cum Laude honor? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that receiving the Summa Cum Laude honor occurs independently and at a constant average rate." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419545 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f608fe3c-6d97-51bc-b0a2-012a45774f2c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419545 #edb/ref 1074 "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of graduate students with the Summa Cum Laude honor. Since the average rate is $5$ graduates with the Summa Cum Laude honor of each group of graduates, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of graduate students can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =5,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(5).$ \n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{5}^{x}{e}^{-5}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that at most $3$ graduate students will have the Summa Cum Laude honor is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in {0,1,2,3}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{{5}^{0}{e}^{-5}}{0!}+\\frac{{5}^{1}{e}^{-5}}{1!}+\\frac{{5}^{2}{e}^{-5}}{2!}+\\frac{{5}^{3}{e}^{-5}}{3!} \\\\  & ≈0.265\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419546 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419527 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419546 #edb/ref 1069 "q-85384" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419546 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419546 #edb/ref 1071 "A bank manager notices that, on average, for each group of  customers, $9$ of them visit the cash machine. What is the probability that in the next group of customers, less than $5$ customers visit the cash machine? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that visits of cash machine occur independently and at a constant average rate." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419546 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f7f3a56e-4b53-5169-b909-9f25434c0fc1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419546 #edb/ref 1074 "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of customers that visit the cash machine. Since the average rate is $9$ customers per group, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of customers that visit the cash machine can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =9,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(9).$ \n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{9}^{x}{e}^{-9}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that less than $5$ customers visit the cash machine in the next group is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 4) & =P(X\\in {0,1,2,3,4}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & =\\frac{{9}^{0}{e}^{-9}}{0!}+\\frac{{9}^{1}{e}^{-9}}{1!}+\\frac{{9}^{2}{e}^{-9}}{2!}+\\frac{{9}^{3}{e}^{-9}}{3!}+\\frac{{9}^{4}{e}^{-9}}{4!} \\\\  & ≈0.055\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419547 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419547 #edb/ref 1069 "q-85375" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419547 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419547 #edb/ref 1071 "The hospital emergency room receives an average of $7$ patients per hour. What is the probability that it receives fewer than $4$ patients in the next hour? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that patients arrive independently and at a constant average rate." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419547 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ff042347-0e79-58c2-92e7-81e42f1921f1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419547 #edb/ref 1074 "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of patients in the hospital emergency room. Since the average rate is $7$ patients per hour, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of patients can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =7,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(7).$ \n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{7}^{x}{e}^{-7}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of having fewer than $4$ patients in the next hour is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4) & =P(X\\in {0,1,2,3}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{{7}^{0}{e}^{-7}}{0!}+\\frac{{7}^{1}{e}^{-7}}{1!}+\\frac{{7}^{2}{e}^{-7}}{2!}+\\frac{{7}^{3}{e}^{-7}}{3!} \\\\  & ≈0.082\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
